<column class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Core concepts</p>
  <h1>Motion</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    One directive, one system. <code>motion:</code> takes a
    <strong>trigger</strong> — when it happens — and a <strong>config</strong> —
    what moves. There is no second animation API to learn, no transition
    directive, and no imperative escape hatch you're expected to graduate to.
  </p>

  <code-block code={code.intro}></code-block>

  <p>
    The values are <strong>presets</strong>, not numbers. A preset is a physical
    description — <code>spring:gentle</code>, <code>fade</code> — and the compiler
    bakes it into the animation at build time. You don't tune a duration; you say
    what kind of movement it is, and the whole app moves the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>Triggers come in three kinds</h2>
  <p>
    The kind matters, because it decides how much JavaScript ends up in your
    frame loop. Two of the three kinds need <em>none</em>.
  </p>

  <h3>Interaction</h3>
  <p>
    <code>hover</code>, <code>press</code>, <code>focus</code>,
    <code>focus-visible</code>. The browser already knows when these happen, so
    the work is deciding what they should look like.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.interaction}></code-block>

  <h3>Lifecycle</h3>
  <p>
    <code>enter</code> and <code>exit</code>. These are the ones a framework
    genuinely has to be involved in: <code>exit</code> holds the element in the
    DOM until its animation finishes, which nothing but the renderer can do — the
    moment you remove a node yourself, its exit animation has nowhere to play.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.lifecycle}></code-block>

  <h3>Timeline</h3>
  <p>
    <code>view</code> and <code>scroll</code> are driven by scroll
    <em>position</em>, not by time. They compile to native CSS
    <code>animation-timeline</code>, which means they run off the main thread:
    <strong>no JavaScript executes while you scroll</strong>. The effect people
    reach for a 30&nbsp;kB animation library to get is a directive with a preset.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.timeline}></code-block>

  <h2>Stagger</h2>
  <p>
    <code>stagger</code> is reserved, not a property: it describes the
    relationship <em>between</em> siblings — the delay step — rather than
    anything about any one of them. It's what makes a list arrive as a list
    instead of a flash.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.stagger}></code-block>

  <h2>The vocabulary</h2>
  <p>
    Small on purpose, and enforced: an unknown trigger or property is a compile
    error, not a silent no-op. A typo used to be something you could ship.
  </p>
  <grid class="grid-2" cols="2">
    <column>
      <h3>Triggers</h3>
      <p class="mono">
        hover · press · focus · focus-visible<br />
        enter · exit<br />
        view · scroll
      </p>
    </column>
    <column>
      <h3>Properties</h3>
      <p class="mono">
        scale · opacity<br />
        translate-x · translate-y<br />
        rotate
      </p>
    </column>
    <column>
      <h3>Springs</h3>
      <p class="mono">
        spring:snappy · spring:gentle<br />
        spring:bouncy · spring:deliberate
      </p>
    </column>
    <column>
      <h3>Tweens</h3>
      <p class="mono">
        fade · fade-out<br />
        press · release · pulse
      </p>
    </column>
  </grid>

  <p class="callout">
    <strong>Reduced motion is not your problem.</strong> Every
    <code>motion:</code> respects <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> at the
    runtime, so honouring it isn't a thing you can forget to do.
  </p>
</column>
